@page

<p>
    <strong>HTML</strong> stands for HyperText Markup Language. You write your words and wrap them
    in <strong>tags</strong> that tell the browser what each part is, like a heading, a paragraph,
    or a link.
</p>

<h2>Parts of a tag</h2>
<pre>&lt;p class="intro"&gt;Hello, world!&lt;/p&gt;</pre>
<ul>
    <li><code>&lt;p&gt;</code> is the <strong>opening tag</strong>. The p means paragraph.</li>
    <li><code>class="intro"</code> is an <strong>attribute</strong>. It adds extra information.</li>
    <li><code>Hello, world!</code> is the <strong>content</strong> that people see.</li>
    <li><code>&lt;/p&gt;</code> is the <strong>closing tag</strong>. The slash means "end here."</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common tags</h2>
<table>
    <tr><th>Tag</th><th>What it makes</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> to <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code></td><td>Headings, from biggest to smallest</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;p&gt;</code></td><td>A paragraph</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;a href="..."&gt;</code></td><td>A link</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;img src="..." alt="..."&gt;</code></td><td>A picture</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;li&gt;</code></td><td>A bulleted list</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;li&gt;</code></td><td>A numbered list</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;strong&gt;</code></td><td>Bold text</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;div&gt;</code></td><td>A box that groups things together</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Tags inside tags</h2>
<p>
    Tags can go inside other tags. This is called <strong>nesting</strong>. Always close the
    inner tag before the outer tag.
</p>
<pre>
&lt;!-- Right --&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I am &lt;strong&gt;very&lt;/strong&gt; excited!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;!-- Wrong --&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I am &lt;strong&gt;very excited!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
</pre>
<p>Lines that start with <code>&lt;!--</code> are <strong>comments</strong>. The browser ignores them.</p>

<h2>A whole page</h2>
<pre>
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;My First Page&lt;/title&gt;
  &lt;/head&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Hello, world!&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;This is my first web page.&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</pre>
<ul>
    <li>The <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> has information about the page, like the title on the browser tab.</li>
    <li>The <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> has everything you see on the page.</li>
</ul>

<p class="note">
    <strong>Why do I see &amp;lt; in the code?</strong> The browser thinks <code>&lt;</code> starts
    a real tag. To show it as text, HTML uses <code>&amp;lt;</code> for &lt; and
    <code>&amp;gt;</code> for &gt;.
</p>

